    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d756">

                <pb facs="1381" n="1359" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg1359"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">786H.02/2–1150: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Saudi Arabia and Minister to Yemen</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Childs</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d756-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-02-11T14:00:00-05:00">February 11,
                            1950—2 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>53. Deptel 21 Jan 19.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d756-f2"/> In view recent recognition Yemen Govt Dept desires early
                    estab friendly relations with Imam Ahmed and Yemeni Govt officials.</p>
                <p>US officials last visited Yemen 1947. Additional visit desirable soonest. Dept
                    suggests you plan visit before commencing anticipated discussions with SAG re DAB<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d756-f3"/> beginning in Apr. McGhee agreeable to extend his
                    stay in your area<anchor n="4" xml:id="d756-f4"/> for purpose accompanying you
                    to Yemen, if you consider desirable and if visit wld not require too much
                    time.</p>
                <pb facs="1382" n="1360" xml:id="pg1360"/>
                <p>Dept understands Fr, Ital and Dutch Mins plan visit Yemen 1950 (A–499 Dec 27<anchor n="5" xml:id="d756-f5"/>). Brit
                    recently informed Dept strong effort wld shortly be made improve Brit relations
                    with Yemen, probably including appt of Trott as Min with residence at Jidda and
                    visit to Yemen this year.</p>
                <p>Dept considers ur appt as Min Yemen still in effect<anchor n="6" xml:id="d756-f6"
                    /> and new credence ltrs unnecessary. However, Dept wld comply if Imam requested
                    but hopes his response ur note seeking entry and audience will not raise
                    question. Dept suggests appropriate officers from Emb Jidda and Con Aden shld also
                    accompany you for purpose attaining objectives Commercial Consular Mission as
                    stated Deptel 194 May 23.<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d756-f7"/>
                </p>
                <p>Pls comment.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d756-f8"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d756-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d756-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Aden.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d756-f2" n="2" xml:id="d756-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed, but see footnote 8, <hi rendition="#italic">supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d756-f3" n="3" xml:id="d756-f3-content">
                <p>For documentation on the discussions concerning Dhahran Airbase, see <ref
                        target="#pg1112">pp. 1112</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d756-f4" n="4" xml:id="d756-f4-content">
                <p>Assistant Secretary of State for Near Eastern, South Asian, and African Affairs,
                    George McGhee, was in Saudi Arabia from March 16 to March 24, following a tour
                    of African countries. Information on his visit to Saudi Arabia is on <ref
                        target="#pg1131">pp. 1131</ref> ff. Regarding his African trip, see the
                    editorial note, <ref target="#pg1512">p. 1512</ref>. There is no evidence that
                    he visited Yemen on his trip.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d756-f5" n="5" xml:id="d756-f5-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d756-f6" n="6" xml:id="d756-f6-content">
                <p>Childs presented his letters of credence as first U.S. Minister to Yemen on
                    September 30, 1946; see note 14 to telegram 69, September 17, 1946, from Jidda,
                        <ref target="frus1946v07#pg_926">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1946, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">vii</hi>, p. 926</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d756-f7" n="5" xml:id="d756-f7-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d756-f8" n="7" xml:id="d756-f8-content">
                <p>In telegram 219, April 18, from Jidda, not printed, Childs reported on a visit he
                    made to Yemen, April 8–16. He told the Imam and other officials that the U.S.
                    Government would welcome the establishment of some form of diplomatic
                    representation in Washington by the Government of Yemen. (786H.02/4–1850)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
